-- IELTS 6.5: Writing topic (graded by Learning Service, no final test)

Those who favour restricting choice argue mainly from the needs of the economy. Countries face shortages of engineers, doctors and data specialists, and public funding for higher education is limited. If taxpayers pay a large share of tuition, it seems reasonable that the money should produce graduates whose skills are in demand. Supporters also point out that graduates in some subjects struggle to find relevant work, so guiding students could protect them from debt and disappointment.

On the other hand, it is difficult to predict which subjects will be useful. Twenty years ago, few people expected that linguists would be needed to train language software or that psychologists would help design online services. Moreover, students who study something they care about tend to work harder and achieve more, and motivation is itself a valuable skill. Societies also need historians, artists and philosophers to question decisions that scientists and engineers make.

In my view, the best approach is to keep freedom of choice while giving students honest information. Universities could publish employment figures for each course and governments could offer extra grants for shortage subjects. In this way, students would make informed decisions without being forced into fields that do not suit them.

In conclusion, although the economic argument for directing students is understandable, the unpredictability of future needs and the value of motivation mean that choice should remain with the student.', 'PUBLISHED');
